.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-POWER 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-power
.Nd switch a machine on, off or through a power cycle from outside it
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar machine
.Cm status
.Nm
.Ar machine
.Cm on
.Nm
.Ar machine
.Cm off
.Op Fl -self
.Nm
.Ar machine
.Cm force-off
.Op Fl -self
.Nm
.Ar machine
.Cm cycle
.Op Fl -self
.Nm
.Ar machine
.Cm pxe-once
.Nm
.Ar machine
.Cm selftest
.Op Fl -cycle
.Op Fl -self
.Nm
.Ar mac
.Cm set
.Fl -controller Cm outlet | ipmi | redfish
.Fl -address Ar host Ns Op : Ns Ar port
.Op Fl -kind Cm shelly1 | shelly2 | tasmota
.Op Fl -outlet Ar n
.Op Fl -user Ar user
.Op Fl -name Ar name
.Op Fl -cycle-off Ar seconds
.Op Fl -allow Ar verbs
.Op Fl -system Ar path
.Op Fl -insecure
.Op Fl -password-stdin
.Nm
.Cm list
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
controls a machine's power without the machine's help: through a smart
plug it is plugged into, or through its baseboard management controller
(BMC) over IPMI or Redfish.
It is what lets a netboot reinstall, a test sweep or an operator recover a
machine that is off, hung, sitting at a passphrase prompt or stuck in an
initrd, none of which answers ssh.
.Pp
A
.Ar machine
is named by one of its MAC addresses, in any case and with
.Ql \&:
or
.Ql - ,
or by the
.Ar name
its record was given with
.Cm set Fl -name .
Each machine has one record, written by
.Cm set
and read by every other verb.
.Pp
Every verb that changes power reads the state back from the controller
until it matches, within a bound, before it reports success.
A reply from a plug or a BMC that the request was accepted is not taken as
evidence that the power changed.
Asking for the state a machine is already in succeeds and says so.
.Pp
Three controllers are supported:
.Bl -tag -width "redfish"
.It Cm outlet
A smart plug with a local HTTP API, chosen by
.Fl -kind :
.Bl -tag -width "tasmota"
.It Cm shelly1
Shelly Gen1:
.Ql GET /relay/ Ns Ar n Ns ?turn=on|off ,
state from the
.Ql ison
field; HTTP Basic authentication when a login is set.
.It Cm shelly2
Shelly Gen2 and later:
.Ql GET /rpc/Switch.Set?id= Ns Ar n Ns &on=true|false
and
.Ql /rpc/Switch.GetStatus ,
state from the
.Ql output
field; SHA-256 digest authentication, user always
.Ql admin .
.It Cm tasmota
Tasmota:
.Ql GET /cm?cmnd=Power Ns Ar n Ns %20On ,
state from
.Ql POWER Ns Ar n
or, on a one-relay plug,
.Ql POWER .
Tasmota takes its password in the URL, so the URL itself is kept off the
command line (see
.Sx CAVEATS ) .
.El
.Pp
A plug switches mains power and cannot shut an operating system down:
.Cm off
is refused and
.Cm force-off
cuts the power.
A plug cannot choose a boot device either, so
.Cm pxe-once
is refused.
.It Cm ipmi
A BMC reached with
.Xr ipmitool 1
over IPMI 2.0
.Pq Fl I Cm lanplus .
.Cm off
asks the operating system to shut down (ACPI soft off),
.Cm force-off
removes power at once, and
.Cm pxe-once
sets the next boot, once, to UEFI network boot and reads the BMC's boot
flags back.
.It Cm redfish
A BMC reached over DMTF Redfish, HTTPS and JSON: the system's
.Ql PowerState ,
its
.Ql ComputerSystem.Reset
action
.Pq Ql On , Ql GracefulShutdown , Ql ForceOff ,
checked against the values the BMC says it allows, and its
.Ql Boot
override
.Pq Ql Pxe , Ql Once , Ql UEFI .
Current Dell and HPE BMCs ship with IPMI over the network turned off and
Redfish on.
.Cm set
pins the public key of the certificate the BMC presents (BMC certificates
are self-signed, so there is no authority to check); a different key later
is refused.
Some BMCs sell Redfish separately: Supermicro X11 boards answer every
Redfish request with a license error, and there
.Cm ipmi
is the controller to use.
.El
.Pp
.Nm
refuses to
.Cm off ,
.Cm force-off
or
.Cm cycle
the machine it is running on (any local interface carrying the
.Ar machine Ns 's
MAC) unless
.Fl -self
is given: a host that cuts its own power cannot report the outcome, and on
a netboot server it takes the rack's provisioning down with it.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--password-stdin"
.It Cm status
Print the power state,
.Ql on
or
.Ql off ,
as one word on standard output.
.It Cm on
Switch the machine on and wait until the controller reports it on.
Through a plug this powers the socket; the machine starts only if its
firmware is set to power on after AC loss.
.It Cm off
Graceful shutdown: an ACPI soft off through the BMC, then a wait of at
least 180 seconds for the machine to report off.
A plug cannot do this; the verb exits 3.
.It Cm force-off
Remove power now, then wait until the controller reports off.
.It Cm cycle
.Cm force-off ,
hold the power off for the record's
.Fl -cycle-off
seconds (10 by default), then
.Cm on .
.It Cm pxe-once
IPMI only: boot from the network on the next boot, once, then back to the
normal boot order.
Pair it with
.Cm cycle ,
or with the next reboot, and arm the machine first with
.Xr kldload-netboot-server 8 .
.It Cm selftest
The acceptance test for one machine's controller, meant for the first box
of a new rack before the rest are trusted.
It reads the power state and the boot override, sets
.Cm pxe-once
and clears it again (each read back, so the machine is left as it was),
and with
.Fl -cycle
cycles the machine, confirms it comes back on and, if it was off, switches
it off again.
Steps the record does not allow, or the controller cannot do, are reported
as SKIP; the report is on standard output and the exit status is 0 only
when nothing failed.
A status-only record runs the two reads and nothing else.
.It Fl -cycle
.Pq Cm selftest
Include the power cycle.
.It Cm set
Create or replace the record for
.Ar mac .
Only a MAC is accepted here; the name is given with
.Fl -name .
.It Cm list
Print every record: MAC, name, controller (with the plug kind) and address.
.It Fl -self
Allow
.Cm off ,
.Cm force-off
or
.Cm cycle
of the machine running the command.
.It Fl -controller Cm outlet | ipmi
.Pq Cm set
How the machine is reached.
.It Fl -address Ar host Ns Op : Ns Ar port
.Pq Cm set
The plug's or the BMC's address.
A port selects a non-default HTTP or IPMI port.
.It Fl -kind Cm shelly1 | shelly2 | tasmota
.Pq Cm set
The plug's API.
Required with
.Fl -controller Cm outlet ,
refused with
.Cm ipmi .
.It Fl -outlet Ar n
.Pq Cm set
The relay on a multi-outlet plug.
Shelly counts from 0 and Tasmota from 1; the defaults are 0 and 1.
.It Fl -user Ar user
.Pq Cm set
The login.
For
.Cm ipmi
the default is
.Ql ADMIN ,
the Supermicro factory user; for
.Cm shelly2
it is ignored, because the API fixes it to
.Ql admin .
.It Fl -name Ar name
.Pq Cm set
A name the machine can be addressed by instead of its MAC.
.It Fl -cycle-off Ar seconds
.Pq Cm set
How long
.Cm cycle
holds the power off.
.It Fl -allow Ar verbs
.Pq Cm set
The only verbs this machine accepts, comma separated, from
.Cm status , on , off , force-off , cycle
and
.Cm pxe-once .
Every other verb exits 6 before the controller is contacted, and nothing
on the command line overrides it; widening it takes a new
.Cm set .
.It Fl -system Ar path
.Pq Cm set , Cm redfish
Which ComputerSystem to drive, as a path such as
.Pa /redfish/v1/Systems/1 ,
for a BMC that manages several.
The default is the first member the BMC lists.
.It Fl -insecure
.Pq Cm set , Cm redfish
Do not pin the certificate.
Every later call then prints a warning, because an unpinned endpoint can be
impersonated and would receive the password.
.It Fl -password-stdin
.Pq Cm set
Read the password as one line from standard input.
Without it the record has no password, which suits a plug with no login.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
Needs no root and has no side effect.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev KLDLOAD_POWER_DIR
Records directory instead of
.Pa /etc/kldload/power .
When set,
.Nm
does not re-execute itself under
.Xr sudo 8 ,
so a test can run it as a user against its own records.
.It Ev KLDLOAD_POWER_LOG
Log file instead of
.Pa /var/log/kldload/power.log .
.It Ev KLDLOAD_POWER_WAIT
Seconds to wait for a state to read back; default 30.
.Cm off
waits at least 180.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/kldload/power/ Ns Ar mac Ns Pa .env
One record per machine, root-owned, mode 0600, in a mode 0700 directory.
Lines are
.Ar KEY Ns = Ns Ar value
and are parsed as data, never sourced:
.Ev POWER_MAC ,
.Ev POWER_CONTROLLER ,
.Ev POWER_ADDRESS ,
.Ev POWER_OUTLET_KIND ,
.Ev POWER_OUTLET ,
.Ev POWER_USER ,
.Ev POWER_PASSWORD ,
.Ev POWER_NAME ,
.Ev POWER_CYCLE_OFF ,
.Ev POWER_ALLOW ,
.Ev POWER_SYSTEM ,
.Ev POWER_TLS_PIN ,
.Ev POWER_TLS .
.It Pa /var/log/kldload/power.log
One line per verb that changed or tried to change power, and per
.Cm set :
time, the invoking user, MAC, name, verb and outcome.
Never the password.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
done, and the state read back matched
.It 1
failed: no record, a malformed record, or the state never read back as
asked
.It 2
usage, or the machine is this host and
.Fl -self
was not given
.It 3
the controller cannot do that verb (a plug:
.Cm off ,
.Cm pxe-once ;
a BMC that does not offer the reset type, or wants a license for Redfish)
.It 4
the controller did not answer
.It 5
the controller refused the login
.It 6
the machine's record does not allow the verb
.Pq Fl -allow
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Every output below was printed by
.Nm
on onyx on 2026-09-29: against abyss's Supermicro BMC, against an IPMI
emulator
.Pq virtualbmc
in front of a throwaway virtual machine, and against a test double of a
Shelly Gen2 plug.
.Ss set: a BMC, status-only (--controller, --address, --user, --name, --allow, --password-stdin)
abyss holds a storage pool that must never lose power by accident, so its
record allows nothing but
.Cm status .
Typing the password at a prompt keeps it out of the shell history and the
process list:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ read -rsp 'BMC password: ' PW; echo
$ printf '%s\en' "$PW" | sudo kldload-power 3c:ec:ef:45:86:c0 set \e
      --controller ipmi --address 10.100.10.76 --user ADMIN \e
      --name abyss --allow status --password-stdin
kldload-power: record written: /etc/kldload/power/3c:ec:ef:45:86:c0.env
$ unset PW
.Ed
.Ss set: a smart plug (--kind, --cycle-off)
fiend on a Shelly Gen2 plug, holding the power off for ten seconds on a
cycle:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf '%s\en' "$PLUGPASS" | sudo kldload-power f0:2f:74:cd:27:50 set \e
      --controller outlet --kind shelly2 --address 10.100.10.40 \e
      --name fiend --cycle-off 10 --password-stdin
.Ed
.Ss set: one outlet of a multi-outlet plug (--outlet)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power 02:00:00:00:00:31 set --controller outlet \e
      --kind tasmota --address 10.100.10.41 --outlet 3 --user admin \e
      --name rack-sw --password-stdin </root/plug.pw
# the record now switches relay 3 (Power3) only
.Ed
.Ss list
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power list
MAC                NAME          CONTROLLER       ADDRESS
3c:ec:ef:45:86:c0  abyss         ipmi             10.100.10.76
.Ed
.Ss status
One word on standard output, so it can be tested in a script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power abyss status
on
$ [ "$(sudo kldload-power fiend status)" = off ] && echo "fiend is off"
.Ed
.Ss on
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power fiend on
kldload-power: the plug is on; the machine starts only if its firmware
powers on after AC loss
$ sudo kldload-power fiend on
kldload-power: fiend is already on
.Ed
.Ss off
Through a BMC the machine is asked to shut down.
The emulated machine had no operating system to answer, and the verb
reported that instead of success:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power ipmiprobe off
kldload-power: asked for off, still on after 180s
$ echo $?
1
.Ed
.Pp
Through a plug it is refused:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power fiend off
kldload-power: a plug cannot shut an OS down; force-off cuts the power
$ echo $?
3
.Ed
.Ss force-off
Prints nothing and exits 0 once the controller reads off:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power fiend force-off
$ sudo kldload-power fiend status
off
.Ed
.Ss cycle
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power fiend cycle
kldload-power: fiend is already off
kldload-power: holding fiend off for 5s
kldload-power: the plug is on; the machine starts only if its firmware
powers on after AC loss
.Ed
.Ss pxe-once
Reinstall a BMC machine that is hung, with no operating system to ssh
into: arm it, send it to the network once, cycle it.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-netboot-server arm-install 02:00:00:00:99:01 answers.env
$ sudo kldload-power ipmiprobe pxe-once
kldload-power: ipmiprobe boots from the network next time, once
$ sudo kldload-power ipmiprobe cycle
.Ed
.Pp
Through a plug it is refused:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power fiend pxe-once
kldload-power: fiend's controller (outlet) cannot choose the boot
device; arm it on the netboot server and cycle it
.Ed
.Ss --allow in force
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power abyss cycle
kldload-power: abyss allows only: status (its record says so; cycle
was not run)
$ echo $?
6
.Ed
.Ss selftest, on a machine that may only be read
abyss's record allows
.Cm status
alone, so the test reads and changes nothing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power abyss selftest
selftest: abyss (ipmi at 10.100.10.76)
  PASS  power state reads: on
  PASS  boot override reads: No override (invalid, once)
  SKIP  pxe-once round trip: the record does not allow pxe-once
  SKIP  power cycle: not asked for (--cycle)
selftest: 2 passed, 0 failed, 2 skipped
.Ed
.Ss selftest --cycle, and a BMC that cannot boot the network once
Against the Redfish emulator, which writes every boot override as
permanent: the test fails that step, undoes it, and says so; the power
cycle passes and the machine is put back off:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power rfprobe selftest --cycle
selftest: rfprobe (redfish at 127.0.0.1:18443)
  PASS  power state reads: off
  PASS  boot override reads: Pxe (Continuous, UEFI)
kldload-power: the BMC took the request but its boot override reads:
Pxe (Continuous, UEFI)
kldload-power: WARNING: the override could NOT be cleared (Pxe
(Continuous, UEFI)): this machine may boot from the network on every
restart until it is fixed on the BMC
  FAIL  pxe-once did not stick; the override now reads: Pxe
(Continuous, UEFI)
  PASS  power cycle: off, held 3s, on again
  PASS  put back off, as it was
selftest: 4 passed, 1 failed, 0 skipped
.Ed
.Ss set: a Redfish BMC (--controller redfish, --system, --insecure)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ printf '%s\en' "$PW" | sudo kldload-power 02:00:00:00:18:43 set \e
      --controller redfish --address 127.0.0.1:18443 --user admin \e
      --name rfprobe --cycle-off 3 --password-stdin
# writes /etc/kldload/power/02:00:00:00:18:43.env; for a Redfish
# record that includes the pinned key, a line of the form
# POWER_TLS_PIN=sha256//<44 characters of base64>
# --system /redfish/v1/Systems/2 picks the second system of a
# multi-node BMC; --insecure skips the pin and warns on every call
.Ed
.Ss A Supermicro BMC without the Redfish license
abyss's BMC, through a temporary status-only Redfish record:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power abyss-rf status
kldload-power: this BMC needs a license for Redfish (SUM DCMS OOB);
use --controller ipmi
$ echo $?
3
.Ed
.Ss --self
Run on onyx, whose own NIC carries the MAC:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ sudo kldload-power onyx force-off
kldload-power: onyx is this machine; --self to cut its power anyway
$ sudo kldload-power onyx cycle --self
# cycles onyx; nothing reports the outcome once the power is cut
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "no power record for 'NAME' in DIR (kldload-power <mac> set ...)"
No record has that MAC or name.
.It "CONTROLLER at ADDRESS did not answer"
The plug or BMC is unreachable: wrong address, powered off, or on another
network.
Exit 4.
.It "CONTROLLER at ADDRESS refused the login"
Wrong user or password.
Exit 5.
A BMC may lock an account after repeated failures; fix the record before
trying again.
.It "asked for STATE, still STATE after Ns"
The controller accepted the request but the state never changed: a
machine that ignored an ACPI shutdown, a plug whose relay did not switch.
.It "the BMC took the request but its boot flags do not say PXE"
.Cm pxe-once
did not stick.
Some BMCs apply a boot override only while the host is off.
.It "NAME allows only: VERBS (its record says so; VERB was not run)"
The record's
.Fl -allow
list refused the verb.
Exit 6.
.It "this BMC needs a license for Redfish (LICENSE); use --controller ipmi"
The BMC refused Redfish with a vendor license error.
Exit 3.
.It "the BMC at ADDRESS presented a different certificate than the one pinned at set"
The certificate's key changed since
.Cm set .
If the BMC's certificate was replaced on purpose, run
.Cm set
again; otherwise something else is answering at that address.
Exit 1.
.It "the override could NOT be cleared (STATE): this machine may boot from the network on every restart"
A BMC took a one-time network boot as permanent and would not take it
back.
Fix it on the BMC before the machine next restarts.
.It "this BMC has no 'no override'; the next boot goes to the disk instead"
Not an error: an IPMI BMC without the standard
.Ql none
setting was sent to the disk, which is the normal boot.
.It "NAME is this machine; --self to cut its power anyway"
Exit 2.
.It "the plug is on; the machine starts only if its firmware powers on after AC loss"
Not an error: a reminder that
.Nm
can see the socket, not the machine behind it.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ipmitool 1 ,
.Xr kfire 8 ,
.Xr kldload-netboot-server 8
.Sh HISTORY
Written on 2026-09-29, the day fiend's live boot crawled at 3 MB/s
fetching its root image, was cut off at 300 seconds, and sat unreachable
in the initrd until someone walked over and pressed the button.
fiend has no BMC; a smart plug gives the same control.
.Pp
Against the IPMI emulator a wrong password and an absent BMC first
produced the same message from
.Xr ipmitool 1 ,
and a refused login was reported as unreachable;
.Nm
has run it verbose since, where the two differ.
The Redfish driver and
.Cm selftest
were added the same evening for a rack in someone else's data center.
The Redfish emulator it was first run against ignores one-time boot
overrides entirely, which is how the first version of
.Cm selftest
was caught hiding a failed clean-up.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
Through a plug,
.Nm
sees the socket, not the machine: a machine whose firmware is not set to
power on after AC loss stays off after
.Cm on
or
.Cm cycle ,
and
.Cm status
reads
.Ql on .
.Pp
.Cm pxe-once
asks for UEFI network boot
.Pq Ql options=efiboot .
The emulator it was tested against ignores that option, so UEFI network
boot through a real BMC has not been demonstrated.
.Pp
.Cm pxe-once
and
.Cm selftest
through Redfish have been run against a test double and against
sushy-tools, and through IPMI against virtualbmc; on real hardware only
the reads (abyss, a Supermicro X11) have been.
Until
.Cm selftest Fl -cycle
has passed on a box of a given make, treat its one-time network boot as
unproven there.
.Pp
The password never appears on a command line: plugs are driven by
.Xr curl 1
reading its URL and user from a configuration on a pipe, and
.Xr ipmitool 1
reads it from its environment
.Pq Fl E .
Anyone who can read the record, or the environment of a running
.Xr ipmitool 1 ,
which on Linux means root, can read the password.
